LABS['foundations'] = ('Foundations and reproducible SQL', '''
<p>Use PostgreSQL 16+ and a dedicated empty teaching database. These exercises support meetings 1, 2, and 5. Start with <a href="setup.sql">setup.sql</a>; it creates only the <code>course32</code> schema and deliberately fails if that schema already exists. For a repeat run, create a fresh teaching database instead of dropping existing work.</p>
<h2>Meeting 1: requirements before syntax</h2><p>A campus currently keeps event registrations in separate spreadsheets. Two staff members can edit seats remaining; a student may register twice; event titles are copied into every row. In pairs, identify three inconsistent states and assign each to a key, foreign key, transaction, or application rule. Explain what a DBMS can enforce and what still requires a precise requirement.</p>
<h2>Meeting 2: setup and integrity lab</h2><p>Budget: 10 minutes predictions, 15 setup demonstration, 15 guided constraints, 50 independent work, 10 debrief.</p>
<ol><li>Run setup.sql. Verify 5 students, 4 events, 6 bookings, and total account balance 200.00.</li><li>Display student names alphabetically; display events with fee at least 20; return event titles and capacity only.</li><li>In separate BEGIN/ROLLBACK blocks, try a duplicate student ID, a booking for student 999, fee -1, and a NULL student name. Record the constraint responsible. After an error issue ROLLBACK before the next test.</li><li>Choose types for money, event dates, email, and capacity. Explain why numeric money and integer capacity express different domains.</li><li>Add one valid student and booking in a transaction, inspect them, then roll back. Verify original counts.</li></ol>
<h2>Meeting 5: query exercises</h2><ol><li>Find students without email. Explain why <code>email = NULL</code> does not answer the question.</li><li>Find names beginning with A, events costing between 10 and 20 inclusive, and bookings in January 2026.</li><li>Predict <code>email IS NULL</code>, <code>email = 'x'</code>, and <code>NOT(email = 'x')</code> for a NULL email.</li><li>Show the two most expensive events with deterministic ordering for ties.</li><li>Raise event 101’s fee by 5 within a transaction; compare before, during, and after ROLLBACK.</li><li>Insert a student whose name contains an apostrophe. Explain SQL escaping now and parameter binding later.</li><li>Write a DELETE that targets exactly one booking; test inside a rolled-back transaction.</li><li>Explain why rows have no guaranteed display order without ORDER BY.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Submit:</strong> SQL, predicted and actual outputs, a four-row constraint log, and corrections. <strong>Stretch:</strong> design tests that distinguish empty string from NULL and missing row from a row containing NULL.</p>
''')
LABS['design'] = ('ER design, dependencies, and decomposition studio', '''
<p>Supports meetings 3, 4, 11, and 12. Use the existing ER and normalization decks for explanations; the following case is a fresh assessment of those concepts.</p>
<h2>Meetings 3–4: Campus Events case</h2><p>A department sponsors many events; every event has one sponsor. An event has one room, start/end times, a fee, and a seat capacity. Students register for many events and each registration records time and payment status. Speakers may appear at many events; each speaking assignment has a position within its event. A speaker can appear at most once in an event. Students cannot register twice for the same event. Events in the same room cannot overlap. A registration cannot exceed event capacity.</p>
<ol><li>Extract entities, candidate keys, attributes, relationship attributes, and min/max cardinalities. State whether an event can have zero speakers and whether cancelled registrations remain in history; these are requirements to clarify, not facts to invent silently.</li><li>Draw the ER diagram; explain the modeling of Registration and SpeakingAssignment.</li><li>Map to relations. Include a composite key for registration and a uniqueness rule for speaker position within an event.</li><li>Map five rules to SQL mechanisms. Explain why an ordinary row CHECK cannot count all registrations safely under concurrency.</li><li>Exchange models: attempt duplicate registration, orphan event sponsor, duplicate speaker position, inverted times, and overlapping room bookings.</li><li>Revise the model and list unresolved requirements separately.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Deliverables:</strong> diagram, schemas, five-rule test matrix, and one-page peer-review response. Each studio uses 10 review + 15 explanation + 15 worked example + 50 design/testing + 10 debrief minutes.</p>
<h2>Meeting 11: functional dependencies</h2><p>Enrollment(S, C, T, StudentName, CourseTitle, Instructor, Office, Grade). T identifies a term. Business rules: S → StudentName; C → CourseTitle; CT → Instructor; Instructor → Office; SCT → Grade. Do not infer any other dependency from sample data.</p>
<ol><li>Compute S+, CT+, and SCT+ with explicit steps.</li><li>Prove that SCT is the only candidate key under these rules.</li><li>Give an update, insertion, and deletion anomaly.</li><li>Explain why two students with the same name do not imply StudentName → S.</li></ol>
<h2>Meeting 12: decomposition and tradeoffs</h2><ol><li>Decompose into Student(S, StudentName), Course(C, CourseTitle), InstructorOffice(Instructor, Office), Offering(C,T,Instructor), and EnrollmentGrade(S,C,T,Grade).</li><li>Check the determinant of every nontrivial dependency in each relation. Prove losslessness by a sequence of binary decompositions whose intersection determines one side. Verify preservation of every listed FD.</li><li>For R(A,B,C) with AB → C and C → B, find all candidate keys. Decide whether R is 3NF and BCNF. Decompose to CB and AC and determine which FD is no longer locally enforceable.</li><li>Apply the same audit to your project. Name one business rule not captured by functional dependencies.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Stretch:</strong> construct a lossy decomposition of R(A,B,C) into AB and BC with no assumed FDs, showing the spurious tuples after joining.</p>
''')
