GUIDE='''<p>Use these checkpoints to assess reasoning, not to require a specific query spelling or optimizer plan. For labs: 40% correct result/design, 30% evidence and edge cases, 20% explanation, 10% reproducibility. This rubric fits inside the existing lab component.</p>
<p><a href="reference-queries.sql">Download worked SQL answers</a> · <a href="validation.txt">Validation record and limits</a></p><h2>Fixture and query checkpoints</h2><ul><li>5 students, 4 events, 6 bookings; accounts total 200.00.</li><li>Roster: Ada–SQL, Ada–Design, Ben–SQL, Cy–Design, Cy–Data, Dee–SQL. Left student roster adds Eli–NULL.</li><li>Booking counts by event: 101=3,102=2,103=1,104=0. Paid counts: 2,1,1,0. Paid revenues: 40,30,30,0; grand total 100.</li><li>No bookings: Eli (5). Both required events: Ada (1). NOT IN against booking_probe returns no students because its rows include NULL. NOT EXISTS against that probe returns 2,3,4,5.</li><li>Classical projection removes duplicate event IDs; SQL SELECT does not unless DISTINCT is specified.</li></ul>
<h2>Design checkpoints</h2><p>S+={S,StudentName}; CT+={C,T,CourseTitle,Instructor,Office}; SCT+ contains all attributes. S,C,T never occur on any FD right-hand side, so every key must contain all three. The proposed five-way decomposition is BCNF for the supplied FDs and preserves them. Each extraction joins through a determinant that is a key of the extracted relation, supporting losslessness.</p><p>For AB→C, C→B, candidate keys are AB and AC. All attributes are prime, so R is 3NF; C is not a superkey, so it violates BCNF. CB/AC is lossless because C→CB, but AB→C is not preserved locally. Example spurious-tuple case with no FDs: R={(a1,b,c1),(a2,b,c2)}; AB join BC adds (a1,b,c2),(a2,b,c1).</p>
<h2>Analytics checkpoints</h2><p>Revenue ranks in event-ID order: RANK 1,2,2,4; DENSE_RANK 1,2,2,3. Deterministic ROW_NUMBER 1,2,3,4. Revenue shares .40,.30,.30,0. Recursive depths: Database=0; SQL,Systems=1; Joins,Transactions=2. A root-only anchor can miss a component with no root; a depth bound and visited path address different concerns.</p>
<h2>Performance checkpoints</h2><p>80 records/page; 1,250 pages; ceil(1250/11)=114 initial runs; merges 114→12→2→1, so three merge passes plus run generation. Total 2×1250×4=10,000 page I/Os under the stated model. Cold point lookup: three index pages plus one heap page = four; cached root reduces new reads by one.</p><p>Page nested-loop: R outer 201,000; S outer 200,200. Block nested-loop: R outer 1,000+50×200=11,000; S outer 200+10×1,000=10,200. Two-pass hash join=3,600, conditional on partitions fitting. Do not grade exact milliseconds or require an index scan on a broad predicate.</p>
<h2>Transactions and operations checkpoints</h2><p>Successful transfer yields 75/125 and total 200. Simplified crash trace recovers A=80,B=120: T1 committed, T2 incomplete. S1 has T1→T2 and is conflict-serializable, but T2 reads T1 before T1 commits; it is recoverable under the listed commit order, not cascadeless. S2 has edges both ways and is not conflict-serializable. Deadlock victim is nondeterministic; both sessions must clean up. READ COMMITTED can see B’s intervening commit on the second read; REPEATABLE READ’s established snapshot does not. A guarded last-item update allows only one decrement.</p><p>Midnight backup / 15:00 failure can lose up to 15 hours of later changes; 40-minute restore exceeds the stated 10-minute objective. A restored table count does not prove identical values. Least-privilege checks must show SELECT events allowed, UPDATE events and SELECT accounts denied.</p>
<h2>Application and project checkpoints</h2><p>Capacity-one event: exactly one of two different-student requests succeeds. Duplicate request produces no extra row. Exceptions before commit leave no booking. Parameter binding preserves quoted text as data. This application-level locking convention requires all writers to participate.</p><p>Midterm debugging fixes: keep paid filter in ON (or conditional aggregate), group by event ID plus title, count the child key, and preserve zero-booking groups. Final demos should show setup, a business query, successful transaction, failure/rollback, one measured optimization, and restore evidence.</p>
<h2>Instructor preparation and boundaries</h2><ul><li>Run the new scripts in a disposable PostgreSQL 16+ database before class; prepare two independent sessions for concurrency.</li><li>Preinstall clients/Python driver and prepare the role-management demonstration before meeting 26.</li><li>Use core tasks for all students and stretch tasks for advanced students. Slow setup should not consume the entire independent-work period.</li><li>Do not change the source lecture PowerPoints just to fit a meeting. The larger decks are resource libraries: select relevant blocks and reserve duplicate examples/hidden slides for reference.</li><li>See validation.txt for embedded PostgreSQL execution checks. Two-session concurrency, Python connectivity, and command-line backup/restore still require a full-server rehearsal.</li></ul>'''
(OUT/'instructor-guide.html').write_text(page('Instructor checkpoints',GUIDE),encoding='utf-8')
print(f'Generated 32 meetings, {len(LABS)} exercise pages, and instructor guide.')

